<!--
A minimal, deliberately ordinary project manifest.

It exists so an eval run has something to answer the skill's first step
with "read the target project's own manifest to determine the
language" is not testable in an empty directory, and an agent that has
to guess the language is not being asked the question the skill
actually poses.

The pinned VerdictRules version matters for the same reason it matters
for every other language: the skill says to fetch deeper documentation
at the version a project actually has, never from the default branch.
Unlike Dart's pubspec.yaml/pubspec.lock pair, a single PackageReference
Version attribute already names the exact resolved version directly
NuGet's default is an exact pin, not a floating range so this
fixture needs no separate lockfile the way Dart's does.

Keep this at a version that is ACTUALLY published and ACTUALLY tagged
(csharp-vX.Y.Z) check NuGet and the git tag before bumping it. A
changelog entry for a version that never went through the real release
workflow is not evidence it exists; pinning one here silently breaks
the fetch-at-the-installed-version instruction this fixture exists to
test.
-->
<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <RootNamespace>StorefrontApi</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="VerdictRules" Version="0.0.1" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
